  "verbalization": "Pitavastatin, Fluvastatin, and Pravastatin are all statin drugs that share a common mechanism of action, binding to Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor, which leads to the inhibition of cholesterol synthesis. This binding is a key pathway that connects these drugs to Cyp2c9, as evidenced by their interactions with the enzyme. The evidence suggests that Pitavastatin and its analogs cause Myoglobin Urine Present or Myoglobinuria, which are conditions that are indirectly linked to Cyp2c9 through the metabolic pathway involving Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor. The lowest common ancestors of these drugs, including Statin (Synthetic) and Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor, provide a structural and functional link that supports the interaction with Cyp2c9. The presence of multiple pathways converging on Cyp2c9, through the shared inhibition of Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor, highlights a recurring mechanism that underpins the relationship between these drugs and the enzyme.",
